\title{sdid: Updated Synthetic Difference-in-Differences Estimation and Inference}
\description{
Synthetic difference-in-differences (Arkhangelsky et al. 2021)
in R, following the Stata package sdid of Clarke, Pailañir, Athey and Imbens
(2024). Start with \code{\link[=sdid]{sdid()}}, then plot results with \code{\link[=sdid_plot]{sdid_plot()}},
\code{\link[=sdid_units_plot]{sdid_units_plot()}} and \code{\link[=sdid_placebo_plot]{sdid_placebo_plot()}}. See the README and the
"Getting started" vignette for examples.

The package builds on synthdid 0.0.9, a prior R implementation of the method
by Dmitry Arkhangelsky, Susan Athey, David A. Hirshberg, Guido W. Imbens and
Stefan Wager.
}
\details{
Package options

\itemize{
\item \code{sdid.num.threads} sets the number of threads used for bootstrap,
jackknife and placebo replications. When unset, all available hardware
threads are used. The \code{num.threads} argument of \code{\link[=sdid]{sdid()}} overrides it.
Results do not depend on the thread count.
\item \code{sdid.solver} selects how the Frank-Wolfe weight solver computes its
gradient. \code{"auto"} (the default) starts with the direct method and
switches to a precomputed Gram matrix once a solve has run as many
iterations as it has weights. \code{"direct"} never switches and reproduces
the results of synthdid 0.0.9 up to rounding. \code{"gram"} uses the Gram
matrix from the first iteration. Estimates with covariates adjusted by
the optimized method always use the direct method.
\item \code{sdid.gram.max.mb} caps the size of the Gram matrix, in megabytes,
defaulting to 64. Solves whose Gram matrix would exceed it use the direct
method.
}
}
